.TH "tum\-close" "1" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
\f[B]tum\-close\f[R] \[em] kill the focused borrowed pane
(context\-aware)
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tum close\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]tum close\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
Destroy the focused borrowed pane with \f[CR]kill\-pane\f[R].
The pane and all its state are gone; there is no cleanup and no
confirmation \[em] close is immediate, mirroring tmuxake.
.PP
Close is context\-aware, sharing the target resolver with \f[B]tum
flip\f[R] and \f[B]tum push\f[R]: if the focused pane is itself
borrowed, close it; if the focused pane is a host with one borrowed
thing, close that; if it is a host with several, open the chooser and
close the selection.
.PP
Close works inside a layout: a closed member is gone from the group and
the remaining members continue with their layout id unchanged.
There is no layout instance to repair \[em] the layout is just an id
stamped on member panes.
Close is destructive; to park a pane without destroying it, use \f[B]tum
flip\f[R] instead.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R] Print a short synopsis (the SYNOPSIS
and EXAMPLES sections of this page) and exit.
For bare \f[B]tum\f[R], list every subcommand.
.SS EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tum close                # focused borrowed pane, or host\[aq]s single, or chooser\-pick
# bound to  t x  in the shipped tum\-bindings.conf (see tum\-keymap(5))
.EE
